    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\nDiscriminated unions pair literal tags with variant-specific fields so control-flow narrowing becomes exhaustive and safe.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nRead the type from left to right, identify what is checked at compile time, then trace one small runtime example.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ntype Result =\n  | {\n      type: \"success\";\n      data: string;\n    }\n  | {\n      type: \"error\";\n      message: string;\n    };\n\nfunction message(\n  result: Result,\n): string {\n  switch (result.type) {\n    case \"success\":\n      return result.data;\n    case \"error\":\n      return result.message;\n  }\n}\n\nconsole.log(\n  message({\n    type: \"success\",\n    data: \"OK\",\n  }),\n);\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / IDIOMATIC TypeScript:\n```ts\nif (result.type === \"success\") {\n  return result.data;\n}\n\nreturn result.message;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\nOK\n```\n\nStep 6 — Complexity / trade-off.\nUse one stable discriminant and avoid overlapping variants.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases.\nCheck null/undefined, unions, widening/narrowing, inference loss, runtime boundaries, incorrect assertions, and API evolution where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway.\nExplain what the compiler proves, what exists at runtime, when the idiomatic/built-in approach is preferable, and the maintainability trade-off.",
